275:## When an edit is refused
276-
277-When the project's rules won't allow an edit, the app **beeps** and nothing changes. The rule's own sentence appears at the **foot of the inspector** after "Refused: ", for example "Refused: clips 4 and 7 would overlap by 120000 ticks, more than half of the shorter clip (100000 ticks)". The message stays until the project changes (an edit, an undo or red
278-
279-An edit that would change nothing, such as a slip already at the end of the footage or a paste of a look the clips already have, beeps too, but shows no message: no rule was broken. A request the project can't use at all, such as a pasted value outside its allowed range, shows an error alert instead.
280-
281-During a drag, positions the project refuses are skipped and the clip waits at the last allowed position. Only the drop counts.
282-
283-*Agents:* an applied edit answers `ok` with a project summary and the ids of anything it created. An edit that changed nothing fails with "… made no change: the project is already in that state." A refused edit fails with "… was refused at <op>: <the rule's sentence>". Neither makes an undo step.
284-
285-## Limits
286-
287-- **The overlap rule.** On one track, a clip may overlap at most one neighbour on each side, and each overlap may be at most half the length of the shorter clip. Moves, trims, nudges, duplicates, gap closes and nests that would break it are refused. Paste and Un-nest put such clips on a new front track instead. Ripple deletes are never refused by it: a c
288-- <a id="overlaps-make-transitions"></a>**Overlaps make transitions.** Any edit that leaves a clip overlapping the clip before it on its track gives it a Cross Dissolve over the overlap, unless it already has a transition: moves, trims, duplicates, pastes, ripple deletes, Close Gap, Nest and Un-nest. See [Motion and transitions](motion-and-transitions.md
289-- **Locked tracks.** Their clips can't be dragged, trimmed, slipped or rolled, and a marquee doesn't select them. Split with nothing selected, Lift, and the range form of Ripple Delete leave them uncut. Ripples, gap closes and inserted time don't shift them. Insert onto a locked track and Close Gap on a locked track are refused. A clip on a locked track 
290-- **One frame minimum.** Trims and rolls always leave a clip at least one frame long.
291-- **Footage bounds.** A media clip can't be trimmed, rolled or slipped past either end of its file.
//! Time: the tick timeline, frame rates, frame snapping, and timecode.
//!
//! Every time in the model is an integer [`Ticks`] at [`TIMESCALE`] per
//! second. 600 000 was chosen because it divides every common frame
//! duration exactly — 24, 25, 30, 48, 50, 60, 120 fps and the /1001 rates
//! (a 30000/1001 frame is 20 020 ticks, a 24000/1001 frame 25 025) — and
//! because `CMTime` at that timescale round-trips without loss.
//!
//! Not every conceivable rate divides exactly, so the snapping math here is
//! written rationally rather than as "multiples of a frame duration": frame
//! boundaries are computed as `t * num / (TIMESCALE * den)` rounded, then
//! mapped back. That is exact for the rates above and still correct (to
//! within a tick, i.e. 1.67 µs) for any other rate a source might carry.
//!
//! This module is the ONE home of tick arithmetic. Nothing else in the
//! crate may re-derive a frame boundary or format a timecode.

use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};

